---
/**
 * /printable-ring-sizer — a paper ring sizer that actually measures correctly
 * when printed.
 *
 * Why this page exists: "printable ring sizer" and "ring size chart printable"
 * are separate searches from the on-screen tool, and they are the ONE case
 * where a competitor already does something right — brite.co prints a check
 * line so the reader can confirm their printer did not scale the page. Most
 * printable sizers do not, which makes them silently wrong by 3-6%: browsers
 * default to "fit to page" and shrink everything.
 *
 * So: match the check line, then beat them on what follows it — a gauge with
 * every size, both a ring-hole gauge and a finger strip, and numbers generated
 * from the same module the rest of the site uses.
 *
 * THE ONE RULE FOR THIS PAGE: every printed dimension is declared in `mm`,
 * never px. A CSS px is 1/96 in only by definition at 96 DPI; print engines
 * scale it. `mm` is honoured as a physical length. If you add anything to the
 * printable sheet and size it in px, it will come out the wrong size and the
 * whole page becomes a liar.
 */
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import FaqSection from '../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { NOINDEX_SITE, SITE_NAME, SITE_URL } from '../config';
import { CHART_ROWS, fromDiameter, diameterFromUs, UK_NOTE } from '../data/ringSizes';
import { CARD_SHORT_MM, CARD_LONG_MM } from '../data/calibration';
import { breadcrumbSchema, PRINTABLE_FAQS, faqPageSchema } from '../data/faq';

/* The check square. 50 mm is chosen so a 30 cm ruler measures it in one go and
   a 1 mm printing error is a visible 2%.

   ⚠️ CHECK_MM is the OUTER dimension of the outline, and the page has to say so.
   Tailwind's preflight sets `box-sizing: border-box`, so `.ps-check`'s width
   INCLUDES its border. Measured out of a real headless-Chrome print-to-PDF
   (31 Aug 2026), the square's WIDTH comes out at:

     outside edge → outside edge   50.006 mm   ← the number the page claims
     stroke centre → centre        49.742 mm
     inside edge → inside edge     49.477 mm

   That is a 0.53 mm spread, against a check whose whole job is to catch a 1 mm
   error. A reader measuring inside-to-inside reads 49.5 and concludes their
   printer is wrong when the sheet is right — the exact false alarm this square
   exists to prevent. Hence "outside edge to outside edge" in both places below;
   do not soften it to just "measure the square".

   Chrome snapped the 0.4 mm border to 1 CSS px, not the 1.51 px declared, which
   is where the inner figure comes from. Only the OUTER figure is guaranteed, and
   only on the axis whose edges happen to land on the pixel grid: see the
   PIXEL SNAPPING note below — the height measured 49.742 mm in the same PDF. */
const CHECK_MM = 50;

/* ── PIXEL SNAPPING — measured, and deliberately not chased ────────────────
   Everything on this sheet is declared in mm, but a browser lays out on a CSS
   pixel grid (1 px = 1/96 in) and snaps box edges to whole device pixels. At
   device scale 1 that quantises every printed dimension by up to 1 px, i.e.
   0.2646 mm. Measured out of the print-to-PDF, 31 Aug 2026:

     check square   width 189 px = 50.006 mm, height 188 px = 49.742 mm
                    (the height moves with where the square lands vertically —
                     it was 189 px before the paragraph above it grew a line)
     ring holes     content diameter = round(d × 96/25.4) px, border 1 px.
                    Worst case over US 3-14 is US 7: 65.47 px asked, 65 px drawn,
                    i.e. -0.124 mm.

   Why this is tolerable rather than a bug:

   - The gauge prints WHOLE US sizes only, which are 0.4064 mm apart in diameter,
     and the half-size boundary is 0.2032 mm away. A 0.124 mm error cannot reach
     it, so no hole can be mistaken for its neighbour. This is a second, quite
     separate reason the "whole sizes only" decision above was right.
   - 0.26 mm is below what a ruler with half-millimetre graduations resolves, so
     the check square still does its job.
   - Device scale 1 is the WORST case. A real printer rasterises finer, so the
     error on paper should be smaller than the figures above, not larger.

   Do not try to fix this in CSS. There is no unit that is both a true physical
   length and an integer number of pixels, and the one attempt that looks
   obvious — nudging sizes onto the grid — would make the numbers wrong on
   purpose to make the rendering tidy. It is measured, bounded, and below the
   resolution of the instrument. Leave it.

   It is written down because it looks exactly like a bug to anyone who measures
   the PDF, and this project has already lost time to two artifacts of that kind
   (frozen CSS transitions in a background tab; a 64.62/64.64 "mismatch" that was
   two different physical values). */

/* The no-ruler fallback. Generated, not typed: the whole point of this page is
   that no printed number is hand-written. */
const CARD_GAP_MM = CARD_SHORT_MM - CHECK_MM;

/* Ring-hole gauge: whole US sizes only. Quarter sizes would put circles 0.2 mm
   apart, which no printer resolves and no reader can distinguish — printing
   them would be precision this medium does not have. The on-screen tool is
   where quarter sizes belong. */
const GAUGE = Array.from({ length: 12 }, (_, i) => fromDiameter(diameterFromUs(3 + i)));

/* Finger strip: the wrap-a-paper-strip method measures CIRCUMFERENCE, so the
   strip is marked in circumference, not diameter. Getting that backwards is
   the classic error in printable sizers.

   The second, subtler error - which this page made on its first draft - is
   sizing the strip by the RANGE of circumferences it reports (33 mm) instead
   of by the DISTANCE the reader wraps. A tick labelled 62 has to sit 62 mm
   from the cut end, or the strip reports nothing at all. So the strip starts
   at zero and runs past the largest size it covers. */
const STRIP = CHART_ROWS.filter((r) => r.usNumeric !== null && Number.isInteger(r.usNumeric));
const STRIP_MAX = Math.ceil(STRIP[STRIP.length - 1].circumferenceMm) + 4;
/* Where real fingers land, for the "you should end up around here" note. */
const STRIP_LOW = Math.floor(STRIP[0].circumferenceMm);
const STRIP_HIGH = Math.ceil(STRIP[STRIP.length - 1].circumferenceMm);

/* "Free PDF-Free Paper Gauge" read as an offer of a free PDF, and there is no
 * PDF anywhere on this page - it IS the printable, printed. Anyone arriving on
 * "printable ring sizer pdf" would have bounced. The no-download fact belongs
 * in the description, where it reads as the advantage it is; the title carries
 * the print-scale check, which is the thing almost no competitor has. */
/* "chart" and "free" are in the title because half this keyword cluster uses
   them - printable ring size chart, ring size chart printable and free
   printable ring sizer are each real searches, and all three are true of this
   page. Kept under 60 characters so nothing is truncated out of the SERP. */
const title = `Free Printable Ring Sizer and Size Chart | ${SITE_NAME}`;
const description =
  `Print a paper ring sizer and ring size chart that measure correctly. A ${CHECK_MM} mm ` +
  `check square proves your printer did not shrink the page. No PDF, no download.`;

const jsonLd = [
  faqPageSchema(PRINTABLE_FAQS),
  breadcrumbSchema([
    { name: 'Ring Sizer', url: SITE_URL },
    { name: 'Printable Ring Sizer', url: `${SITE_URL}/printable-ring-sizer` },
  ]),
];
---

<Layout title={title} description={description} noindex={NOINDEX_SITE} jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <main id="main" class="mx-auto w-full max-w-4xl px-6 py-12 sm:px-8 sm:py-16">
    <h1 class="font-display text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-text">
      Printable ring sizer
    </h1>

    <p class="mt-4 max-w-[62ch] text-text-muted leading-relaxed">
      Print this page, check the square with a ruler, then measure. The check square is the part
      that matters: browsers shrink pages to fit the paper by default, and a sizer printed 4%
      small reads about a size and a half too tight. Every printable sizer that skips this step
      is guessing.
    </p>

    <!-- Print instructions: screen only. On paper they would waste the space
         the gauge needs, and the reader is already holding the sheet. -->
    <section class="no-print mt-8 rounded-2xl border border-border bg-surface-sunk/40 p-6">
      <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-text">Before you print</h2>
      <ol class="mt-3 list-decimal space-y-2 pl-5 text-text-muted leading-relaxed">
        <li>
          In the print dialog set <strong class="text-text">Scale</strong> to <strong class="text-text">100%</strong> — not &ldquo;Fit to page&rdquo;, not
          &ldquo;Shrink to fit&rdquo;. This is the single step that decides whether the sheet is
          accurate.
        </li>
        <li>Use A4 or US Letter. Either works; the gauge fits both.</li>
        <li>
          Measure the printed square with a ruler — <strong class="text-text">outside edge to outside edge</strong> of the outline, not inside to inside. Every side must be exactly {CHECK_MM} mm. If it is not, your
          printer scaled the page — fix the scale setting and print again rather than trying to
          correct for it.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-text-faint">
        Nothing is uploaded and there is no PDF to download — this is the page itself, printed.
      </p>
      <button
        type="button"
        data-print
        class="mt-5 inline-flex min-h-11 cursor-pointer items-center rounded-full bg-accent-bright px-6 font-semibold text-on-accent transition-opacity hover:opacity-90"
      >
        Print this sheet
      </button>
    </section>

    <!-- ── The printable sheet ──────────────────────────────────────────────
         Everything below is sized in mm and must stay that way. -->
    <div class="print-sheet mt-10">
      <!-- 1. Scale check -->
      <section class="ps-block">
        <h2 class="ps-h">1 · Check the scale first</h2>
        <p class="ps-p">
          Measure this square from the <strong>outside</strong> of the outline to the outside of the outline opposite — not inside to inside, which reads about half a millimetre small. Every side must be exactly {CHECK_MM} mm. If it is not, the sheet
          is scaled and every size below it will be wrong.
        </p>
        <div class="ps-check-wrap">
          <div class="ps-check" aria-hidden="true"></div>
          <p class="ps-check-label">{CHECK_MM} mm × {CHECK_MM} mm, outside edge to outside edge</p>
        </div>
        <p class="ps-p ps-note">
          A ruler is enough. If you do not have one, every bank card in the world is the same size under ISO/IEC 7810: {CARD_LONG_MM.toFixed(2)} mm long and {CARD_SHORT_MM.toFixed(2)} mm wide. Hold the card&rsquo;s short edge along one side of the square — the square should fall {CARD_GAP_MM.toFixed(2)} mm short of it.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- 2. Ring-hole gauge -->
      <section class="ps-block">
        <h2 class="ps-h">2 · Measure a ring you already own</h2>
        <p class="ps-p">
          Lay a ring over the circles. The right one is the circle whose outline just disappears
          inside the ring&rsquo;s inner edge. If the ring sits between two, take the larger.
        </p>
        <ul class="ps-gauge">
          {GAUGE.map((g) => (
            <li class="ps-gauge-item">
              <span class="ps-hole" style={`width:${g.diameterMm}mm;height:${g.diameterMm}mm`} aria-hidden="true"></span>
              <span class="ps-gauge-label">
                <strong>US {g.us}</strong>
                <span class="ps-gauge-sub">
                  {g.diameterMm.toFixed(1)} mm · UK {g.uk ?? '—'} · EU {g.eu ?? '—'} · JP {g.jp ?? '—'}
                </span>
              </span>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </section>

      <!-- 3. Finger strip -->
      <section class="ps-block ps-break">
        <h2 class="ps-h">3 · Or measure your finger</h2>
        <p class="ps-p">
          Cut along the strip. Wrap it around the base of your finger, snug but not tight, and
          read the millimetre mark where the end meets the strip. That figure is your
          circumference — find it in the table below.
        </p>
        <div class="ps-strip-wrap">
          <div class="ps-strip" style={`width:${STRIP_MAX}mm`}>
            {/* Tick at n mm sits exactly n mm from the left-hand (cut) end.
                That is what makes the strip a ruler rather than a decoration. */}
            {Array.from({ length: STRIP_MAX + 1 }, (_, mm) => {
              const major = mm % 5 === 0;
              const inRange = mm >= STRIP_LOW && mm <= STRIP_HIGH;
              return (
                <span
                  class={`ps-tick${major ? ' ps-tick-major' : ''}${inRange ? ' ps-tick-live' : ''}`}
                  style={`left:calc(${mm}mm - var(--ps-strip-border))`}
                  aria-hidden="true"
                >{major ? <span class="ps-tick-num">{mm}</span> : null}</span>
              );
            })}
            <span class="ps-strip-start" aria-hidden="true">Start wrapping from this end</span>
          </div>
          <p class="ps-check-label">
            {`Circumference in millimetres, measured from the left end · cut along the outline · most adult fingers land between ${STRIP_LOW} and ${STRIP_HIGH} mm`}
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="table-scroll">
        <table class="ps-table">
          <caption class="ps-p">Circumference to ring size</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Circumference</th><th>US</th><th>UK</th><th>EU</th><th>JP</th><th>Diameter</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {STRIP.map((r) => (
              <tr>
                <td>{r.circumferenceMm.toFixed(1)} mm</td>
                <td><strong>{r.us}</strong></td>
                <td>{r.uk ?? '—'}</td>
                <td>{r.eu ?? '—'}</td>
                <td>{r.jp ?? '—'}</td>
                <td>{r.diameterMm.toFixed(1)} mm</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
        </div>
        <p class="ps-p ps-note">{UK_NOTE}</p>
      </section>
    </div>

    <!-- Screen-only: send people to the tool that does not depend on a printer -->
    <section class="no-print mt-12 border-t border-border pt-8">
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-text">No printer?</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 max-w-[62ch] text-text-muted leading-relaxed">
        The on-screen sizer calibrates against a bank card instead of a printer, which removes the
        scaling problem entirely, and it carries quarter sizes that paper cannot resolve.
      </p>
      <a
        href="/"
        class="mt-4 inline-flex min-h-11 items-center font-semibold text-accent-text underline underline-offset-2 hover:opacity-80"
      >
        Measure on screen instead →
      </a>
    </section>
    <div class="no-print mx-auto w-full max-w-4xl px-4 sm:px-6">
      <FaqSection faqs={PRINTABLE_FAQS} heading="Printable ring sizer questions" id="faq" />
    </div>

  </main>
</Layout>

<style define:vars={{ checkMm: `${CHECK_MM}mm` }}>
  /* ── The printable sheet ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
     EVERY physical dimension here is mm. Do not convert any of it to px, rem
     or any relative unit: this block's whole purpose is that a printed
     millimetre is a real millimetre. Screen rendering is the secondary case
     and is allowed to look approximate. */

  .print-sheet { color: var(--color-text); }

  .ps-block { margin-bottom: 12mm; }
  .ps-h {
    font-family: var(--font-display, serif);
    font-size: 5mm;
    font-weight: 600;
    margin-bottom: 2mm;
  }
  .ps-p {
    font-size: 3.4mm;
    line-height: 1.55;
    max-width: 150mm;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    margin-bottom: 3mm;
  }
  .ps-note { font-size: 3mm; color: var(--color-text-faint); }

  /* 1 · scale check */
  .ps-check-wrap { margin: 4mm 0; }
  .ps-check {
    /* Driven from CHECK_MM, never typed — the prose and the square must not be
       able to disagree about the one number this page is built on. */
    width: var(--checkMm);
    height: var(--checkMm);
    /* box-sizing is border-box (Tailwind preflight), so this border sits INSIDE
       the 50 mm: outside edge to outside edge is CHECK_MM. That is what the copy
       above tells the reader to measure. Do not switch to content-box without
       changing the copy too. */
    border: 0.4mm solid var(--color-text);
    /* No border-radius: a square with rounded corners cannot be measured
       edge-to-edge with a ruler, which is the entire point of it. */
  }
  .ps-check-label { font-size: 3mm; color: var(--color-text-faint); margin-top: 1.5mm; }

  /* 2 · ring-hole gauge */
  .ps-gauge {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 4mm 0 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 6mm 8mm;
  }
  .ps-gauge-item { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 3mm; }
  .ps-hole {
    display: block;
    flex: none;
    border: 0.3mm solid var(--color-text);
    border-radius: 50%;
    /* The BORDER marks the ring's inner edge, so it must be thin and the box
       must be content-box: with border-box the stroke would eat into the
       diameter and every hole would read small. */
    box-sizing: content-box;
  }
  .ps-gauge-label { font-size: 3.2mm; line-height: 1.4; }
  .ps-gauge-label strong { display: block; font-size: 3.6mm; }
  .ps-gauge-sub { color: var(--color-text-faint); font-size: 2.8mm; }

  /* 3 · finger strip */
  .ps-strip-wrap { margin: 9mm 0 6mm; }
  .ps-strip {
    /* The reader cuts along the outline, so distances must be measured from the
       OUTSIDE of it. `left` positions against the padding edge, which is inside
       the border, so every tick is pulled back by exactly the border width. */
    --ps-strip-border: 0.3mm;
    position: relative;
    height: 12mm;
    border: var(--ps-strip-border) dashed var(--color-text-muted);
    box-sizing: content-box;   /* same reason as .ps-hole */
  }
  .ps-tick {
    position: absolute;
    top: 0;
    width: 0;
    height: 3mm;
    border-left: 0.2mm solid var(--color-text);
  }
  .ps-tick-major { height: 6mm; border-left-width: 0.3mm; }
  /* Ticks outside the plausible human range stay on the strip - the reader may
     genuinely land there - but recede, so the eye goes to the useful span. */
  .ps-tick { opacity: 0.45; }
  .ps-tick-live { opacity: 1; }
  /* Above the strip, not inside it: at bottom:1mm this collided with the tick
     numbers, which sit at top:6.5mm on a 12 mm strip. */
  .ps-strip-start {
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    bottom: calc(100% + 1mm);
    font-size: 2.6mm;
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--color-text-faint);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
  .ps-tick-num {
    position: absolute;
    top: 6.5mm;
    left: 0;
    transform: translateX(-50%);
    font-size: 2.6mm;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  /* table */
  .ps-table { border-collapse: collapse; font-size: 3mm; margin-top: 4mm; }
  .ps-table caption { text-align: left; }
  .ps-table th, .ps-table td {
    border: 0.2mm solid var(--color-border);
    padding: 1mm 2.5mm;
    text-align: left;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }
  .ps-table th { font-weight: 600; }

  @media print {
    /* Real page geometry. Without an @page rule the browser applies its own
       margins, which differ per browser and shift nothing that matters — but
       stating it means the sheet is identical everywhere. */
    @page { size: A4 portrait; margin: 12mm; }

    /* Ink, and contrast: the gauge outlines must survive a cheap printer, so
       force black on white rather than trusting the theme variables, which may
       be mid-grey or (in dark mode) near-white on near-black. */
    .print-sheet, .print-sheet * { color: #000 !important; background: #fff !important; }
    .ps-check, .ps-hole, .ps-tick, .ps-tick-major { border-color: #000 !important; }
    .ps-strip { border-color: #555 !important; }
    .ps-table th, .ps-table td { border-color: #000 !important; }

    /* A horizontal scroll container has no meaning on paper and would clip the
       table at the page edge instead of letting it lay out. */
    .table-scroll { overflow: visible !important; }

    /* Nothing may split across a page break mid-measurement. */
    .ps-block { break-inside: avoid; }
    .ps-break { break-before: page; }
    .ps-gauge-item, .ps-strip-wrap, .ps-table tr { break-inside: avoid; }
  }
</style>

<script>
  document.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-print]')
    ?.addEventListener('click', () => window.print());
</script>
